  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Legal and institutional environment enabling the full realization of social dialogue and the necessary conditions for decent and productive workplaces</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Serbia has made progress in establishing social dialogue mechanisms, but challenges remain in ensuring their effective functioning. To strengthen these processes, the ILO is working to enhance the Economic and Social Council and improve the regulatory and institutional framework for collective bargaining and dispute resolution. Through a comprehensive analysis of Serbia&#x2019;s social dialogue landscape, including lessons from EU member states, the ILO supports regulatory reforms and capacity-building initiatives. This includes providing technical input on key labour legislation, such as the Law on Peaceful Settlement of Labour Disputes and the new Laws on Economic and Social Council and Strike. 
The ILO is also raising awareness among policymakers, employers, trade unions, and local government on the benefits of social dialogue and collective bargaining. Training and peer-learning programs will equip stakeholders with skills to engage effectively in these processes. 
In response to Germany&#x2019;s 2021 Supply Chain Due Diligence Law, the ILO is improving access to grievance mechanisms for Serbian workers in German supply chains. It promotes internal grievance procedures, provides training for company compliance officers, and strengthens trade unions&#x2019; ability to support workers. Legal departments of trade union confederations are also being equipped with tools to offer legal advice on grievance mechanisms. 
Additionally, the ILO is supporting Serbia&#x2019;s Labour Inspectorate in harmonizing occupational safety and health (OSH) legislation and modernizing inspector training. A situational analysis of the Labour Inspectorate informs capacity building efforts.</narrative>
  </description>
